1
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:08,560
The StitchSwitch primitive is bistable 
consisting of a pop up state and a pop  

2
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:18,800
down state that is easily actuated and remains 
stable in either state without continuous force.

3
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:24,800
We embroider triangle tessellated patterns onto 
pre-stretched elastic fabric locally stiffening  

4
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:30,000
the stitched regions. When released, the 
surrounding fabric contracts while the embroidered  

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,520
region resists, creating a tension mismatch that 
drives the surface out of plane. The symmetric  

6
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:41,920
tessellation distributes these forces evenly. We 
parameterize each patch by its fill percentage  

7
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:48,240
and diameter. As patch diameter increases, the 
overall deformation grows in height. As fill  

8
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:53,440
percentage increases, the silhouette shifts from 
smooth dome-like curvature to flatter centers with  

9
00:00:53,440 --> 00:01:00,240
sharper edges. Below a threshold of size and fill, 
patches remain nearly flat. They no longer exhibit  

10
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:06,160
bistable behavior. This allows us to create 
designs like this with a constrained bistable  

11
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:12,640
region within a non-bistable non-circular bounding 
shape. We introduce a data-driven simulation  

12
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:18,080
model that predicts deformation from patch 
parameters. Starting from a flat initialization,  

13
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:23,920
the model minimizes elastic energy, producing 
puckering and converging to a stable profile.  

14
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,640
It represents the fabric as radial strips with 
higher stiffness in the embroidered region,  

15
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:32,960
capturing both stretching and bending. 
Across the design space, it predicts  

16
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:40,560
resulting height and silhouette. We use this 
to drive the UI with predictive simulation.

17
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:44,320
We provide an interactive design tool 
that supports a full workflow from  

18
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:48,400
layout to fabrication. Users can load 
garment panels and place StitchSwitch  

19
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:53,440
patches directly onto the fabric layout. 
Each patch is parameterized by diameter and  

20
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:58,880
fill percentage with a linked 3D view providing 
real-time preview of the resulting deformation.  

21
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,920
The system also supports inverse design, allowing 
users to specify target deformation and retrieve  

22
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:09,520
compatible parameter combinations. In fabrication 
mode, the tool automatically arranges patches into  

23
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:15,040
embroidery hoops based on available hoop sizes 
and checks constraints such as hoop fit, spacing,  

24
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:20,720
and minimum feature size. If constraints are 
violated, users are guided back to design mode to  

25
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:31,120
adjust parameters. Final designs can be exported 
directly as machine compatible embroidery files.

26
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,680
Using the design and hoop assignments from the UI,  

27
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:38,000
we prepare the fabric panel and stretch 
it uniformly and secure it in a hoop with  

28
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,320
stabilizer. We stitch the generated patterns 
directly onto the fabric. After stitching,  

29
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:55,360
the fabric is released and the stabilizer is 
removed. We do this again with the second hoop.

30
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:01,920
The stabilizer is thoroughly washed away and 
our programmed deformation emerges. We integrate  

31
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:07,600
StitchSwitch directly into garments. Here, a 
bistable embroider patch forms a deformation-based  

32
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:13,200
pocket for a tennis skirt to hold a tennis ball 
securely without requiring additional seams or  

33
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,720
inserts. This demonstrates how deformation can 
be used in garment construction with embedded  

34
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:23,760
functionality. The tessellation also enables 
programmable placement of conductive thread for  

35
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:29,200
sensing. We demonstrate sensing in our second 
application. Motivated by low vision users,  

36
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:34,880
we design a programmable garment interface. We 
integrate tactile buttons and braille labels  

37
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:42,400
directly into the fabric, encoding 
easy access to common shortcuts.

